add='''## Facts used in Charter, District 4 (the Board report)
Checked on 20 November 2026 with WebFetch and WebSearch (a direct download of the NIST PDF is refused by this environment's proxy, so each string was checked by WebFetch, with control strings that must be, and were, reported absent). Everything else in the Board report is invented and labelled (game) in the Field Manual: the report's rows and wordings, the one-page limit, the annex's records and their times, the grading and the report score, the members' questions, Ilka's contracts status of Day 85, the sign-in counts, and the Rolling Hitch Badge. Every line is filled from the player's own records. The records are the Compact's own; no technique, ATT&CK ID or attack research is used. No law is named. The Quiet Thread appears only as one open item, built from the notes in the series record (the five Crossroads notes already cited, T1021.001, T1018, T1083, T1049 and T1087, are not repeated or extended), with no conclusion.
- NIST, CSF 2.0 Implementation Examples (26 February 2024), https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/2024/02/21/CSF%202.0%20Implementation%20Examples.pdf, fetched 20 November 2026, twice:
  - GV.OV-01 "Cybersecurity risk management strategy outcomes are reviewed to inform and adjust strategy and direction"; its Ex1 "Measure how well the risk management strategy and risk results have helped leaders make decisions and achieve organizational objectives". EXACT.
  - GV.OV-03 "Organizational cybersecurity risk management performance is measured and reviewed for adjustments needed"; its Ex3 "Collect and communicate metrics on cybersecurity risk management with senior leadership". EXACT.
  - Controls reported absent: GV.OV-03 with "evaluated" for "measured" (the wording in the design note CAREER_LADDER.md §3, which is wrong); "Collect and report metrics on cybersecurity risk management to senior leadership"; Ex2 of GV.OV-03 with "organisation" and without "potential".
  - Also seen, not used: GV.OV-02 and its examples.
- CISA NICCS, task T1060, https://niccs.cisa.gov/tools/nice-framework/task/t1060, fetched 20 November 2026: "Advise senior management on organizational cybersecurity efforts", EXACT; listed under OG-WRL-007 Executive Cybersecurity Leadership (and OG-WRL-001, -003, -008, -014). Control "Advise the board on organizational cybersecurity efforts": absent. Already in the NICE fact since slice 1; re-verified for this district.
- Recalled, quoted from the game's own records (grepped): the Day 67 minute's sentence (budget-story.js, now built by chBudgetMinuteParts in risk.js, the same text); the acceptances (decidedBy, signedBy, signedFor); "Staff records aren't in the appetite's list either. Both go to the Board's Day 155 review" (suppliers-story.js, Day 74 11:50); "Inkmere signs on Thursday, with the clauses your plan priced" (story.js); EX-01 and EX-03 closed by Edda on Day 78 at 09:50 and 11:10, and board-display's 08:31 and 08:34 sign-ins (policy-story.js, policy-data.js); 138 minutes to 10 (budget-data.js, drill B); the Quiet Thread notes' times and FD-STAFF-11's address (editions/crossroads/quiet.js: 03:12 to 03:16, 03:21 to 03:22, 03:14, 03:11, 03:13); Rae Okafor, "suspicion-file lead" (Crossroads data.js).
- Name searched (WebSearch, 20 November 2026), invented for this district:
  - Adopted: **the Rolling Hitch Badge** (a knot that holds a line against a pull along it, as a report holds to its records). Searched three times: (1) "Rolling Hitch" with company, software, security, game or app: no company, product or game of that name (nearest: generic "Hitch" towing and rideshare apps, RollingHot LLC); (2) "Rolling Hitch Badge" or "Rollinghitch" with video game, character, anime or film: nothing of that name (nearest: the anime The Rolling Girls, a different name); (3) "Rolling Hitch" with cybersecurity, GRC, compliance or risk software: only general GRC vendor lists, no product of that name.
  - Dropped before use: "Plimsoll" (Plimsoll Publishing, a publisher of company-analysis reports, and Plimsoll Productions, too near in a reporting district); "Clove Hitch" (Cloverhitch Technologies, a software company); "Carrick Bend" (Carrick Capital Partners, an investor in software companies); "Lead Line" (a LEADLINE trademark on record); "Binnacle" (search inconclusive, not pursued).
  - No other new name: the record IDs in the annex (APP, M67, M74, M81, X74, LOG, SCO, SUP, SGN, ACC, EXC) are generic.

'''
